  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 254 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper level low continues to build into the region \ntoday, bringing a couple more rounds of showers. The most widespread \nshowers look to be in the afternoon and early evening as a broken \nline pushes down from the north. These will again be convective, and \nwith temperatures aloft continuing to cool, a bit of graupel or pea \nsized hail will be possible out of the strongest showers. Shorter \nstorms will make thunder unlikely, though it cannot be completely \nruled out. Snow levels look to drop to around 4,000 feet before the \nprecipitation exits this evening, so a couple flurries/snow showers \nare possible in the highest peaks. While hypothetical snow levels \nwill continue to fall tonight, any sufficient moisture capable of \nproducing precipitation will have exited, though a few lingering low \nclouds could produce rime ice. Surface high pressure builds into the \nregion for tomorrow, leading to mostly sunny skies, lightening winds \nand high temperatures in the 50s for most places. Relatively \nwidespread frost is likely tomorrow morning, but winds look to \nremain elevated for a longer portion of Friday night, so its extent \nin the Champlain looks somewhat limited and uncertain, though it \ncould be enough for a Frost Advisory to be issued today. High \npressure pushes to the east for Sunday and return southerly flow \nwill cause temperatures to rise into the 60s. High clouds ahead of \nIsaias will infiltrate the region during the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The center of the remnants of Isaias will track well to \nour south Sunday night into Monday morning, but the precipitation \nshield will extend far north so showers and a period of stratiform \nrain are expected away from the international border. Totals ranging \nup to a half inch are currently expected, with the highest values \nfarther south. There will likely be a sharp gradient on the northern \nend and where this exactly sets up remains uncertain. The rain \nshould move out for most of the day monday though.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: A low pressure system will track north of our forecast \narea, and a surface cold front drops south across our area on \nTuesday. This front crossing the area brings our next chance for \nshowers to the region. Following this frontal passage temperatures \nwill trend below normal for a couple days.",
